FAIRNESS 9 objection(s) · 3 serious
========================================================================

  [NEW · SERIOUS · THIRD_PARTY] The draft attributes specific enumerated omissions to MLQ News but MLQ News does not appear in any verifiable source, making the characterisation of their coverage unverifiable.
     quote         : “MLQ News enumerated what was missing: hazard ratios, confidence intervals, p-values, median follow-up, event counts.”
     why           : This is a specific factual claim about a named outlet's editorial conduct — that it listed five specific missing data items. MLQ News does not appear in any search result or in the draft's source list. If the outlet did not enumerate all five items, or if the outlet does not exist under that name, the sentence is wrong and attributes conduct to a third party without evidence. A wronged outlet would be right to object.
     fix           : Either add MLQ News to the source list with a link to the specific article, quote the enumeration directly, or remove the attribution and describe the enumeration without naming the outlet.

  [NEW · MINOR · FACT] The draft says 'A week on, no numbers have been released' but the piece was updated on 2 September, two weeks after the 19 August announcement.
     quote         : “A week on, no numbers have been released.”
     why           : The original publication was 26 August (one week after the announcement), so 'a week on' was accurate at first publication. After the 2 September update, the same sentence now reads as if only one week has elapsed when it has been two. A reader arriving on 2 September is misled about the timeline.
     fix           : Change to 'As of this update, no numbers have been released' or 'Two weeks on, no numbers have been released' and update as needed.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · FACT] The draft makes a significant factual claim about adjuvant PD-1 inhibitors and overall survival without citing any source.
     quote         : “For adjuvant PD-1 inhibitors specifically — the comparison arm in this very trial — no randomised trial against placebo has reported a statistically significant overall survival benefit as a prespecified endpoint in its own population, though indirect comparisons have suggested a signal.”
     why           : This is a sweeping claim about the entire adjuvant PD-1 inhibitor evidence base. It is presented as established fact, but no source is cited for it anywhere in the piece or source list. If it is wrong — if any such trial has reported a prespecified OS benefit — the piece has made a false factual claim in a clinical context where it matters. The absence of a citation is the problem, not necessarily the claim itself.
     fix           : Add a citation to a systematic review, meta-analysis, or the specific trial records that support this claim, or qualify it as the authors' reading of the literature with a note that readers should verify.

  [NEW · MINOR · THIRD_PARTY] The draft characterises FierceBiotech as having attributed the figures correctly, but provides no link, quote or date for FierceBiotech's coverage.
     quote         : “The outlets we reached attributed those figures correctly to KEYNOTE-942 — among them The ASCO Post, Dermatology Times, OncLive and FierceBiotech”
     why           : The ASCO Post and OncLive coverage is verifiable from search results and consistent with the characterisation. FierceBiotech is named but does not appear in the source list and cannot be verified. Naming an outlet as having done something correctly is still a claim about their conduct; if FierceBiotech's article did not attribute correctly, the sentence is wrong.
     fix           : Add FierceBiotech to the source list with a link, or remove it from the named list and keep the characterisation general.

  [NEW · MINOR · THIRD_PARTY] The draft characterises Dermatology Times as having stated that 'detailed Phase 3 efficacy metrics were not disclosed,' but Dermatology Times does not appear in the source list and the specific quote cannot be verified.
     quote         : “Dermatology Times that detailed Phase 3 efficacy metrics were not disclosed.”
     why           : This is a specific claim about what a named outlet wrote. Dermatology Times is named in the body but does not appear in the source list. If the outlet did not use that characterisation, the attribution is wrong. A named outlet that did not write what is attributed to it would be right to object.
     fix           : Add Dermatology Times to the source list with a link and the specific passage, or remove the named attribution and describe the point without naming the outlet.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says 'blinded against the assessment bias the earlier one carried,' implying the Phase 3 is fully protected against that bias, when the draft's own body immediately notes the Phase 3's primary endpoint is investigator-assessed.
     quote         : “It is the more trustworthy trial by a wide margin — seven times larger, and blinded against the assessment bias the earlier one carried.”
     why           : The phrase 'blinded against the assessment bias' implies the bias is eliminated. The very next paragraph explains that the Phase 3's primary endpoint is 'assessed by the investigator' and that an independent review committee would be needed to remove clinical judgement from the endpoint. The sentence is not false — blinding substantially reduces the bias — but 'blinded against' implies a completeness the draft itself qualifies. This is the same issue the correction log says was fixed by removing 'exactly,' but the residual phrasing still overstates.
     fix           : Change to 'blinded against most of the assessment bias the earlier one carried' or 'substantially protected against the assessment bias the earlier one carried,' consistent with the qualification in the paragraph that follows.

  [NEW · MINOR · THIRD_PARTY] The draft says 'Neither reading reached the general coverage' about the Morning Glory Sciences and Pharmacy Times arguments on stage IIB/IIC risk, but offers no evidence that general coverage was checked for this point.
     quote         : “Neither reading reached the general coverage.”
     why           : This is a claim about what general coverage did not contain — a claim about omission across an unspecified set of outlets. The draft does not say which general outlets were checked or how many. 'Nobody reported X' is a claim about behaviour that requires instances. If any general outlet did cover this point, the sentence is wrong.
     fix           : Change to 'Neither reading appeared in the general coverage we reviewed' and specify which general outlets were checked, or remove the claim about general coverage entirely.

  [NEW · MINOR · FACT] The draft describes the April 2023 source of the one-sided p-value as a 'press release' in the body but the source list calls it 'first detailed KEYNOTE-942 results,' which was an AACR conference presentation, not a press release.
     quote         : “The April 2023 press release reported KEYNOTE-942 as one-sided p = 0.0266.”
     why           : The AACR 2023 abstract (CT001) is the primary source for the one-sided p = 0.0266 and was a conference presentation, not a press release. The source list labels it 'first detailed KEYNOTE-942 results' without calling it a press release. Calling it a press release in the body mischaracterises the document type, which matters in a piece about the difference between press releases and peer-reviewed data.
     fix           : Change 'The April 2023 press release' to 'The April 2023 AACR presentation' or 'The April 2023 company announcement' to match the source list and the actual document type.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · THIRD_PARTY] The draft attributes a specific argument to Morning Glory Sciences — including a direct quote — but Morning Glory Sciences does not appear in the source list and cannot be verified from any search result.
     quote         : “Morning Glory Sciences: the Phase 2b population was stage IIIB–IV, the Phase 3 adds node-negative disease, and 'absolute recurrence risk in that group is lower, so the same hazard ratio delivers a smaller absolute benefit.'”
     why           : A direct quote is attributed to a named outlet. Morning Glory Sciences appears in the body's source attribution but not in the formal source list, and returns no results in search. If the outlet did not publish this argument in these words, the attribution is wrong. A direct quote attributed to a named outlet that did not write it is a correction-level error.
     fix           : Add Morning Glory Sciences to the source list with a URL and confirm the quote against the original article, or remove the quotation marks and paraphrase with a note that the source is held.

