COVERAGE 11 careful treatment(s) elsewhere · 4 contradiction(s), 1 serious
========================================================================

  Practical Dermatology
     got right     : Explicitly stated that 'The companies did not disclose hazard ratios, absolute event rates, P values, or other phase 3 efficacy estimates in the topline announcement' — the most complete single-sentence enumeration of what was missing found in any outlet. Correctly attributed the 49%/59% figures to the Phase 2b KEYNOTE-942 trial. Noted that OS and other secondary endpoints remain under evaluation.
     still omitted : No discussion of the open-label design of KEYNOTE-942 versus the double-blind Phase 3, no mention of the stage IIB/IIC population expansion and its implications for absolute benefit, no explanation of what a hazard ratio is or why its absence matters, no discussion of the OS interval width or the exploratory/descriptive status of the survival analysis, and no note on the one-sided versus two-sided p-value distinction.
     https://practicaldermatology.com/news/intismeran-plus-pembrolizumab-meets-dual-phase-3-melanoma-endpoints/2488176/

  Dermatology Times
     got right     : Stated explicitly that 'Detailed phase 3 efficacy metrics (e.g., HRs) were not disclosed' and that 'OS and other secondary endpoints remain immature.' Correctly attributed the 49% and 59% figures to the Phase 2b KEYNOTE-942 five-year follow-up, not to INTerpath-001. Noted the double-blind design of the Phase 3.
     still omitted : No discussion of the open-label weakness of KEYNOTE-942, no mention of the stage IIB/IIC population expansion, no absolute event rates or RFS percentages, no explanation of the OS interval or its width, no note on the descriptive-only status of the five-year analyses, and no discussion of the p-value framing difference.
     https://www.dermatologytimes.com/view/personalized-mrna-based-melanoma-vaccine-meets-primary-endpoints-in-landmark-phase-3-trial

  OncLive
     got right     : Correctly attributed the 49% and 59% figures to the Phase 2b KEYNOTE-942 five-year follow-up data presented at ASCO 2026, with full hazard ratios and confidence intervals, clearly framed as context for the Phase 3 rather than as Phase 3 results. Cited the trial registry record (NCT05933577).
     still omitted : No statement that Phase 3 efficacy numbers were not released, no discussion of the open-label versus double-blind design difference, no mention of the stage IIB/IIC population expansion, no OS interval or survival rates, no note on the descriptive status of the five-year analyses.
     https://www.onclive.com/view/intismeran-autogene-plus-pembrolizumab-meets-rfs-dmfs-end-points-in-resected-melanoma

  The ASCO Post
     got right     : Correctly attributed the 49% and 59% figures with full HRs and CIs to the Phase 2b KEYNOTE-942 trial presented at ASCO 2026, not to INTerpath-001. Described the Phase 3 as double-blind and placebo- and active-comparator-controlled. Noted the trial enrolled 1,137 patients.
     still omitted : No explicit statement that Phase 3 efficacy numbers were withheld, no discussion of the open-label weakness of KEYNOTE-942, no mention of the stage IIB/IIC population expansion, no OS interval or survival rates, no note on the descriptive status of the five-year analyses, and no p-value discussion.
     https://ascopost.com/news/august-2026/interpath-001-trial-of-mrna-based-individualized-neoantigen-therapy-meets-primary-and-key-secondary-endpoints-in-patients-with-high-risk-resected-melanoma/

  Morning Glory Sciences
     got right     : Explicitly stated that Phase 3 hazard ratios have not been disclosed and therefore the Phase 2b figures 'cannot be placed alongside the 1,137-patient result' and 'belong to the Phase 2b population, not to this one.' Correctly identified the stage IIB/IIC population expansion and argued that lower absolute recurrence risk in that group means 'the same hazard ratio delivers a smaller absolute benefit.' Noted the open-label design of KEYNOTE-942 versus the double-blind Phase 3.
